<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav I of Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1496">1496</a>–<a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Gustav Vasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav I, known as Gustav Vasa, was a Swedish nobleman who led the revolt against Christian II after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm-bloodbath">Stockholm Bloodbath</a> of 1520 and was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1523, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. He broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, confiscated church property and introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and in 1544 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> made the crown hereditary in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">Vasa dynasty</a>. In 1556 he granted the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> to his son John.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Gustav I of Sweden" width="312" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacob Binck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustav_Vasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
